"""Hand-built journey specification for 'There and Back Again'.

Every journey records who, when, where, how sure we are (grade + reason), whether
they came back, and an ordered list of waypoints. Each waypoint names the place,
where its coordinates come from, an approximate date, the leg (out/back), a
confidence (known / approximate / conjectural) and the Wikipedia article and
section that mentions it.

Waypoint tuple:  (place label, coordinate key, date, leg, confidence, source section, note)
  coordinate key:  "Title"          -> title in wikipedia_journeys_link_coords.json
                   "gn:Name:CC"     -> GeoNames cities1000 (largest match in country CC)
                   "txt:lat,lon"    -> a position quoted in the article text itself
Drawn routing points (not stops) are written ("~", lat, lon, leg): they only shape a
sea or land path between two real waypoints and are always marked "drawn".

Dates: "YYYY-MM-DD", "YYYY-MM", "YYYY" or "c. YYYY"; negative years are BC.
"""

G_PROVEN, G_PROBABLE, G_DISPUTED, G_LEGEND = "Proven", "Probable", "Disputed", "Legend"

J = []


def add(**k):
    J.append(k)


